A||||3||batboy||Hey Ratt, rear sway bar question||||1||
Z||000000||batboy||12-03-1999||07:50 PM||||I found a website quite a while ago where the guy said he mounted a front Fiero sway bar onto the rear of his Fiero, but there were no details. Recently, you mentioned that you've done this too. I'm very interested. Do you mind explaining what needs to be done in order to accomplish this mod?||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||12-06-1999||11:07 AM||||I wonder what happened to Ratt? Hope he didn't get caught in a trap or ate by a cat. Still out there Ratt?||||reg||1
Z||000002||ratt||12-06-1999||04:16 PM||||Traps! Cats! Yikes! And I thought this was a safe place to visit.  Sorry I didn't reply sooner...I don't have much time to play these days & I usually visit here weekdays during lunch.  Anyways, lets get the legalities out of the way.  I don't claim to be a mechanical engineer or professional mechanic, just a backyard fiero hobbiest who likes to tinker so any mods suggested here are use at your risk.  To mount a front sway bar on the rear is fairly simple.  I've done it to my wifes 85se & my 86se.  Both rear bars have been on the cars for 2-3yrs now (cars are driven everyday, all year) and there have been no complications.  The handling improvement is very noticable.  First, get your hands on a fiero front sway bar c/w the frame bushings & caps from the wreckers.  For the end links, buy a poly endlink kit for an early 80's camaro or firebird (these also fit the front sway bar so you should by a second kit & do the front if you haven't already - these are cheaper than buying them from fiero suppliers).  You will need to buy 2 - 3/8 x 3 1/2" long bolts, 4 - 5/16 x 1" long bolts c/w nuts, 2 lock washers & 4 flat washers.  You will also need some flat bar (I used aluminum, 1/4" thick x 1" wide x 3" long - 2 pieces) for spacers - your friendly neighbourhood welding shop probably has something kicking around.  You are also going to need car ramps, a hydraulic jack, sockets & wrenches to fit the above bolts & end link nuts, & an electric drill with 3/8 & 5/16 bits.  Ok, I think we're ready (bear with me as I'm going by memory).  Put the fiero on the ramps (back end of course).  The sway bar is going to go from the control arms to the rear of the car with the frame bushing brackets being mounted on the engine cradle just before where the e-brake cables pass through.  Take bushing bracket (or cap) & place it lengthwise along the engine cradle with the first bolt hole approx. 1/2" away from the e-brake cable.  Mark & then drill 2 5/16" dia. holes for the bracket (you should notice an opening in the engine cradle frame on the side which will allow you access to the bolts).  Do the same on the other side.  Also drill matching holes in your flat bar spacers as these go between the engine cradle & the brackets to drop the bar enough to clear the cradle.  For now, I just used the stock bushings here but poly would be better.  Now mount the sway bar & frame bushings c/w flat bar spacers to the cradle frame.  The bolt is placed with the head outside the frame.  Put a flat washer under the bolt head.  Wind on the 5/16 nuts & l/washers & remaining flat washers.  Do not tighten these down just yet - just snug them up.  Now rotate the sway bar ends up towards the control arms.  The end should hit on a nice little flat surface on the control arm.  Hold the bar in this position & drill a 3/8" dia. hole through the control arms, (don't allow the bar to slide sideways).  The bolt supplied with the endlink kit isn't used because it is to long for the rear application so this is where the 3/8" bolts are used.  Take 2 poly bushings & cut them in half so they are only half the thickness they were originally.  Put 2 whole poly bushings & caps on both sides of the sway bar & run your 3/8" bolt up through them & through the control arm.  Place the cut poly bushing & its cap on the top side of the control arm.  The bolt will not quite go through so place a hydraulic jack under the bolt & compress the bushings a little to allow enough thread to show to wind on the endlink kit nut.  I used the 3 1/2" long bolts because any longer bolt would get a bit to close to the rubber axel boot.  Now tighten everything up & check that the bolts clear the axel boots.  If all is well then take the car for a spin & see what you think.  I powder coated my sway bar red to match the car, looks cool.  Anyways, I'm sure things can be done to improve on this installation ie: use poly sway bar frame bushings,  replace the engine cradle mounting bushings with aluminum or poly, etc.<BR>you could probably buy the 1" front swaybar & mount it on the back using this method (probably a better idea for those with heavier than stock engines).  Well thats it.  If you do this remember to look everything over under the car to understand the installation & make sure your engine cradle frame has openings on the side near where the e-brake goes through.  Hopefully I haven't left anything out.  Happy driving.||||reg||10
Z||000003||batboy||12-06-1999||05:09 PM||||Thanks Ratt! I printed a hard copy of your instructions for later when I find another sway bar and finally get around to mounting it on the car. I got so many things going on right now, it might be a while before I get a chance to try this mod. I already have poly front sway bar bushings and end links, so I'll probably get matching ones for the rear too. Thanks again.||||reg||1
